BLOCK|h1|1|Chapter 14: High-Performance Site Generation & Native Release Workflows
BLOCK|p||Cluster-lang is built from the ground up to offer two distinct advantages: **microsecond site generation speeds** and **zero-dependency standalone compiler binaries**. This chapter details the design of the Cluster Site Engine, compares it to legacy JavaScript/Ruby tools like Astro and Jekyll, and explains the native compilation workflow.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|1. Native Compiler Releases (Zero-Dependency Workflow)
BLOCK|p||Normally, compiling Cluster-lang scripts utilizes the local developer compilation command (`cluster <file.cl>`). However, in production and production-ready developer machines, installing Python, Lark, and LLVM is unnecessary.
BLOCK|p||Through the automated release workflow configured in [release.yml](file:///home/alamgir-zk/Cluster-Family/cluster-family-builds/cluster-lang-release/.github/workflows/release.yml), every release push automatically compiles the runner into standalone, single-file native executables:
BLOCK|list||**Linux:** `cluster-v0.1-linux`
BLOCK|list||**Windows:** `cluster-v0.1-windows.exe`
BLOCK|list||**macOS:** `cluster-v0.1-macos`
BLOCK|h3|3|Standalone Execution:
BLOCK|p||Once downloaded, you do not need Python or external dependencies. You compile and execute Cluster-lang scripts directly using:
BLOCK|code|bash|# Compile and execute natively in one step
./cluster-v0.1-linux main.cl
BLOCK|p||The standalone binary packages the parser, transpiler, and LLVM bindings internally, directly invoking the system compiler (`g++` or `clang++`) to build raw machine-code binaries (`.out`) instantly.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|2. The Hybrid Site Engine Architecture
BLOCK|p||The Site Engine uses two custom modular file extensions:
BLOCK|p||1. **`.cld` (Cluster Data):** Dedicated content source files containing frontmatter-style metadata (such as titles, authors, and dates) separated by `---` from the article body.
BLOCK|p||2. **`.cltp` (Cluster Template):** Reusable HTML page structures.
BLOCK|quote||[!NOTE]
BLOCK|quote||The template extension is **`.cltp`** (Cluster Template) rather than `.clt`, because **`.clt`** is strictly reserved for database schema table definitions (e.g., `users.clt` connected via `clt_connect()`).
BLOCK|code|mermaid|graph TD
    A[Article Data: .cld] -->|Transpiles to Struct| C[C++ Compiler]
    B[Page Layout: .cltp] -->|Transpiles to Layout Fn| C
    C -->|Compiles to| D[Native Executable: .out]
    D -->|Serves instantly| E[Client Web Browser]
BLOCK|h3|3|Type-Safe Data Binding:
BLOCK|p||A `.cld` data source transpiles into a type-safe Cluster `model` representation. For example, `article.cld` automatically generates:
BLOCK|code|python|model ContentData:
    title: string
    date: string
    author: string
    category: string
    body: string

fn get_data() -> ContentData:
    ...
BLOCK|p||This ensures that templates bind variables at compile-time with 100% type safety, preventing layout syntax errors at runtime.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|3. Speed & Performance: Cluster vs. Astro & Jekyll
BLOCK|p||Modern site generators like **Astro** (JavaScript/Node.js) and **Jekyll** (Ruby) are popular, but they operate under major runtime constraints.
BLOCK|table||| Feature | Astro (Node.js) | Jekyll (Ruby) | Cluster Site Engine (C++) |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Language** | JavaScript / TypeScript | Ruby | Cluster-lang (Compiles to C++) |
| **Runtime Dependency** | Node.js Engine / V8 VM | Ruby Virtual Machine | **None** (Native Bare-Metal OS) |
| **Build & Render Phase** | File System build / hydration | Liquid transpilation loops | **Direct C++ header translation** |
| **Startup Speed** | ~500ms - 2s | ~1s - 3s | **< 2 microseconds** |
| **Memory Footprint** | ~150MB - 300MB | ~80MB - 150MB | **< 8MB** |
| **Core Web Vitals / SEO** | High (but has JS overhead) | High (Static files only) | **Maximum** (Instant first byte SSR) |
BLOCK|h3|3|Why Cluster-lang Outperforms Astro & Jekyll:
BLOCK|p||1. **Zero Virtual Machine overhead:** Astro requires the heavy V8 JavaScript Engine, and Jekyll requires the Ruby interpreter. These virtual machines consume significant RAM and require warmup periods. The compiled Cluster `.out` binary runs directly on the CPU with zero runtime interpreter layers.
BLOCK|p||2. **Bare-Metal SSR (Server-Side Rendering):** In Astro, server-side rendering is performed by Node.js, which dynamically evaluates JS files. In Cluster, page assembly is written directly as inline C++ string concatenations compiled into binary assembly instruction codes, letting the server stream HTML responses to clients instantaneously.
BLOCK|p||3. **Optimized Build Speed:** Traditional generators parse files, build a virtual DOM, hydrate templates, and write static files back to the disk. Cluster transpiles templates directly into standard C++ compiler headers. During compilation, the C++ compiler compiles the source directly, resulting in an ready-to-run `.out` binary in seconds.
BLOCK|hr||
BLOCK|h2|2|4. Fully Working Example
BLOCK|h3|3|Content File (`article.cld`)
BLOCK|code|text|title: "Cluster Site Engine"
date: "July 9, 2026"
author: "Zk Developer"
category: "Release"
---
This content is compiled directly into a C++ model.
BLOCK|h3|3|Layout Template (`news_layout.cltp`)
BLOCK|code|html|<component name="NewsLayout" title: string, author: string, date: string, category: string, body: string>
    <html>
        <head><title>{title}</title></head>
        <body style="background: #09090e; color: #fff; font-family: sans-serif; padding: 40px;">
            <span style="color: #00ffcc;">{category}</span>
            <h1>{title}</h1>
            <p>By {author} on {date}</p>
            <div>{body}</div>
        </body>
    </html>
</component>
BLOCK|h3|3|Application Entrypoint (`main.cl`)
BLOCK|code|python|import "article.cld" as article
import "news_layout.cltp" as tmpl

fn main():
    art_data := article.get_data()
    
    // Bind data to template and run native web server
    html := tmpl.NewsLayout(
        art_data.title,
        art_data.author,
        art_data.date,
        art_data.category,
        art_data.body
    )
    serve_web(8080, html)
BLOCK|hr||
